  "recommendations": [
    "Use exact location as a strong feature and candidate source, not a hard global filter: 16.9% of train positive pairs use different ids.",
    "Build city centers from unique item metadata medians over supplied corpus and permitted training items. Never treat numeric id differences as distances.",
    "For search locations absent from item metadata, infer geographic scope from training clicked item locations only. In validation fit this fallback on the training fold and exclude validation target pairs.",
    "Broad search locations can cover many cities. Use empirical search-location to item-location probabilities plus distances, and expose count/dispersion confidence features instead of forcing one city center.",
    "Retrieve independent semantic or lexical pools from exact location, nearby cities, and the entire corpus. Tune fusion weights and candidate counts on grouped validation, not fixed per-query hardcoded answers.",
    "Candidate distance features: log1p kilometers, exact id match, unknown center indicator, within 25/50/100 km, empirical location transition probability with smoothing. Attenuate distance for broad query regions or remote services.",
    "Treat filter-to-microcategory as a smoothed empirical prior. Broad service labels span many microcategories, so hard microcategory exclusion can lose recall.",
    "Benchmark filters are substantially sparser than train; ensure text-only queries and empty filters receive adequate validation weight."
  ],
